oup gets together overcomes their  fears and takes down a demonic clown so  why does this formula work in the first  film but not the second well we think it  has to do with the way structure and  timing work both in horror and in movies  at large as the master of girls Alfred  Hitchcock said there is no terror in the  bang only in the anticipation of it and  it's precisely this understanding of  timing or lack thereof that makes  chapter one so good and Chapter two so  bad so let's dive in  to screenwriting guru John York a  properly constructed scene should  structurally mirror your archetypal  three-act film structure to York  storytelling is ultimately fractal in  nature kind of like a pattern that  repeats itself regardless of the scale  in his book into the woods  he writes stories as a whole illustrate  complete change acts show major change  and scenes of minor individual moments  it's in the latter these single cells of  the organism that a writer's effort is  concentrated for each is a unit of  change and just as a movie is usually  told in three acts so can a scene  usually be divided into three main parts  first our protagonist wants to do  something only to be thwarted by another  character or force of antagonism before  a choice is made and one of those two  opposing forces gives way now it's  important to note that this tripartite  structure isn't a hard and fast rule  frankly there are as many ways to  formulate a scene as there are fast and  furious movies that being said we found  that a good scare can usually be broken  down in this way while a bad one usually  rejects it to see this in action though  we need to examine some key scenes from  both movies perhaps the most iconic  scene of both films is the opening scare  for chapter 1 in this iconic moment we  see the three-part structure pretty  clearly first let's start with the  action Georgie wants his boat back to  that end he answers penny wisest  seemingly harmless questions about his  life a