ror  of course just when it seems Eddy's  about to go the way of young Georgie he  finds a hole in the gate and escapes  though undoubtedly scarred for life  but while chapter one is clearly  competent at structuring scares chapter  two is quite a bit Messier let's  breakdown the scene when grown-up Ricci  returns to his childhood arcade to  collect his token while there he has a  flashback in which he recalls playing  video games with another kid only to  have that kid call him out in front of  Bowers as gay why are you being weird  I'm not a boyfriend  then Bowers shouts Ritchie out of the  arcade leaving him to wander the park oh  right and Ritchie then gets chased by a  demonic lumberjack statue if you're  wondering why this part wasn't  particularly terrifying it's not because  the brawny towel dude isn't a little  frightening  it's because the film doesn't build into  the scare none of the action in the  arcade Prime's us for the scare so when  our lumberjack comes out swinging it  seems pretty much out of nowhere but  it's not like this lack of an effective  three-part structure is  isolated event in Chapter two whether  it's Bill reliving his trauma or Eddie  going for round two with the leper each  scene reaches a scare in the  turning-point stage that has nothing to  do with the antagonist in the action or  reaction stages for example let's look  at when Ben reminisce is about hiding  out in a classroom with Beverley - a  small false scare here the conflict the  scene revolves around been wanting to  tell Beverley his true feelings we have  the action in which Ben is worried about  how they'll get on then the reaction  which Beverley says it should be firing  as long as the two of them can still  hang out then decision time is the  turning point as Ben decides to lean in  for the kiss and gets rejected and  that's where the film attempts to get  scary out of nowhere Beverley decides to  go all Ghost Rider which leads to been  sharing a locker with penny wi